The final match of the [[1994 FIFA World Cup]], the 15th edition of the quadrennial [[Association football|soccer]] competition organized by [[FIFA]] for the men's national teams of its member associations, took place at the [[Rose Bowl (stadium)|Rose Bowl]] in [[Pasadena, California|Pasadena]], [[California]], United States, on July 17, 1994. [[Brazil national football team|Brazil]] beat [[Italy national football team|Italy]] 3–2 on [[Penalty shoot-out (association football)|penalties]] to claim its fourth World Cup title when the game finished 0–0 after [[Overtime (sports)#Association football|extra time]].<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.planetworldcup.com/CUPS/1994/final_bra_v_ita.html|title=1994 FIFA World Cup Final Details |work=Planet World Cup |access-date=October 11, 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20040622141516/http://www.planetworldcup.com/CUPS/1994/final_bra_v_ita.html |archive-date=June 22, 2004 |url-status=live }}</ref> This was the first time Brazil lifted the [[FIFA World Cup Trophy]]; for their previous three titles, last won in [[1970 FIFA World Cup|1970]], they were awarded the [[Jules Rimet Trophy]]. This is also the first FIFA World Cup final held in the United States, with the second being the [[2026 FIFA World Cup final|2026 final]].

It is to date the only World Cup final to be scoreless after 120 minutes, and the first to be decided by a penalty shoot-out. [[FIFA World Cup awards#Golden Ball|Silver Ball]] winner [[Roberto Baggio]] missed the decisive penalty for Italy.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Divine by moniker, divine by magic: Roberto Baggio|publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |website=FIFA.com|access-date=June 1, 2014|url-status=dead|archive-date=June 2, 2014 |url=http://www.fifa.com/classicfootball/players/player=174363/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140602200053/http://www.fifa.com/classicfootball/players/player=174363/}}</ref> Brazil had previously beaten Italy in the [[1970 FIFA World Cup final|1970 final]], marking the 1994 final as the second time that the same teams had met in two different World Cup finals, after [[Argentina national football team|Argentina]] and [[Germany national football team|West Germany]] met in [[1986 FIFA World Cup final|1986]] and [[1990 FIFA World Cup final|1990]].<ref>{{cite web |last=Bevan |first=Chris |date=May 27, 2010 |title=The Story of the 1994 World Cup |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/blogs/chrisbevan/2010/05/the_story_of_the_1994_world_cu.html |work=BBC Sport |access-date=October 11, 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100530090316/https://www.bbc.co.uk/blogs/chrisbevan/2010/05/the_story_of_the_1994_world_cu.html |archive-date=May 30, 2010 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="coming">{{cite news |first=John F. |last=Molinaro |title=1994 World Cup: Coming to America |url=http://www.cbc.ca/sports/soccer/1994-world-cup-coming-to-america-1.779022 |publisher=CBC Sports (CBC/Radio-Canada) |date=November 21, 2009 |access-date=February 26, 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140328081245/http://www.cbc.ca/sports/soccer/1994-world-cup-coming-to-america-1.779022 |archive-date=March 28, 2014 |url-status=live }}</ref> Played at 12:30&nbsp;pm local time, this was the last World Cup final to be played in broad daylight until the 2026 final.

FIFA awarded the final game of the tournament to the famous college sports stadium near [[Los Angeles]] on June 30, 1992; the Rose Bowl was the largest stadium used for the tournament.<ref>{{cite news | url=https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-1992-06-30-sp-1347-story.html | title=Rose Bowl Gets Final of World Cup | date=June 30, 1992 | first=Rone | last=Tempest | newspaper=Los Angeles Times | access-date=March 8, 2019 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150402174924/http://articles.latimes.com/1992-06-30/sports/sp-1347_1_world-cup-ticket | archive-date=April 2, 2015 | url-status=live }}{{Subscription required}}</ref> With over 94,000 spectators, the 1994 final is the most recent World Cup final to have an attendance of 90,000 or more as of 2026.

The Brazilian team dedicated its win to the late triple [[Formula One]] motor racing world champion [[Ayrton Senna]], who had [[Death of Ayrton Senna|died]] in an accident at that year's [[1994 San Marino Grand Prix|San Marino Grand Prix]] in Italy two and a half months before.<ref>{{cite news |first=Alex |last=Bellos |title=Brazil still in thrall to the Senna legend |url=https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2004/apr/22/formulaone.comment |work=The Guardian |publisher=Guardian News and Media |date=April 22, 2004 |access-date=February 26, 2015 }}</ref> With Italy finishing as runner-up, [[Franco Baresi]] became the sixth player in history to win gold, silver, and bronze medals at the FIFA World Cup.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Milan and Italy's imperial sweeper: Franco Baresi|publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |website=FIFA.com|access-date=January 10, 2014 |url=http://www.fifa.com/classicfootball/players/player=174375/ |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140110162108/https://www.fifa.com/classicfootball/players/player=174375/|url-status=dead|archive-date=January 10, 2014}}</ref> Previously this result was attained in 1974 by five German players: [[Sepp Maier]], [[Franz Beckenbauer]], [[Wolfgang Overath]], [[Jürgen Grabowski]], and [[Horst-Dieter Höttges]].

==Background==
{{See also|Brazil–Italy football rivalry}}[[File:1994 - Questra (USA).jpg|thumb|An example of the Adidas Questra ball used in the game|alt=View of the game ball]]
The [[1994 FIFA World Cup]] was the 15th edition of the [[FIFA World Cup|World Cup]], [[FIFA]]'s competition for national teams, held in the United States between June 17 and July 17, 1994.<ref name="Britanica">{{Cite encyclopedia |url-status=live |title=World Cup: History & Winners |last1=Tikkanen |first1=Amy |last2=Augustyn |first2=Adam |last3=Levy |first3=Michael |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210828023709/https://www.britannica.com/sports/World-Cup-football |archive-date=August 28, 2021 |last4=Ray |first4=Michael |last5=Luebering |first5=J. E. |last6=Lotha |first6=Gloria |last7=Young |first7=Grace |last8=Shepherd |first8=Melinda C. |last9=Sinha |first9=Surabhi |last10=Rodriguez |first10=Emily |encyclopedia=[[Encyclopædia Britannica]] |date=August 19, 2021 |access-date=September 22, 2021 |url=https://www.britannica.com/sports/World-Cup-football }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=World awaits cup draw |author= |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=December 4, 1997 |access-date=October 29, 2021 |url= https://news.bbc.co.uk/2/hi/sport/36829.stm |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20021101074559/http://news.bbc.co.uk/1/hi/sport/36829.stm |archive-date=November 1, 2002 |url-status=live }}</ref> The finals featured 24 teams, with the [[United States men's national soccer team|United States]] qualifying for the finals automatically as tournament host, along with [[Germany national football team|Germany]] as the [[1990 FIFA World Cup|1990 winner]].<ref name="1994qualifying">{{cite web |title=World Cup 1994 Qualifying |date=December 30, 2019 |access-date=April 22, 2022 |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tables/94q.html |first1=Karel |last1=Stokkermans |first2=Sergio Henrique |website=[[RSSSF]] |last2=Jarreta |archive-date=April 26, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250426171948/https://www.rsssf.org/tables/94q.html |url-status=live }}</ref> The remaining 22 spots were decided through [[1994 FIFA World Cup qualification|qualifying rounds]] held between March 1992 and November 1993, organized by the six [[List of football federations#FIFA Confederations|FIFA confederations]] and involving 147 teams.<ref name="1994qualifying"/><ref>{{cite book |title=Historical Dictionary of Soccer |first=Tom |last=Dunmore |edition=illustrated |publisher=Scarecrow Press |year=2011 |page=109 |url=https://books.google.com/books?id=9j1wbp2t1usC&pg=PA109 |isbn=978-0-81087-188-5}}</ref> In the final tournament, the twenty-four teams were divided into six groups of four with each team playing each other once within the group in a [[round-robin tournament|round-robin]] format. The two top teams from each group, along with the four best third-placed sides, advanced to a [[Single-elimination tournament|knockout phase]].<ref name="RSSSF_summary">{{Cite web |title=World Cup 1994 |last1=Stokkermans |first1=Karel |last2=Jarreta |first2=Sergio Henrique |date=July 5, 2018 |access-date=April 22, 2022 |website=[[RSSSF]] |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tables/94f.html |archive-date=July 16, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210716090616/http://www.rsssf.com/tables/94f.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

The game was played at the [[Rose Bowl (stadium)|Rose Bowl]], in the city of [[Pasadena, California]], part of the [[Los Angeles metropolitan area]].<ref name="LATimesRoseBowl">{{Cite web |title=Rose Bowl Gets Final of World Cup |last=Tempest |first=Rone |work=[[Los Angeles Times]] |date=June 30, 1992 |access-date=April 22, 2022 |url=https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-1992-06-30-sp-1347-story.html |archive-date=April 2, 2015 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150402174924/http://articles.latimes.com/1992-06-30/sports/sp-1347_1_world-cup-ticket |url-status=live }}</ref> With a capacity of 102,000, it was the largest of the venues selected for the World Cup and had previously hosted the [[Football at the 1984 Summer Olympics|gold medal match at the 1984 Summer Olympics]] and five [[Super Bowl]]s, as well as the annual [[Rose Bowl Game]] in [[College football|college American football]].<ref name="LATimesRoseBowl"/><ref>{{Cite web |title=Rose Bowl Stadium: The Story of an L.A. Icon |work=Discover Los Angeles |date=August 21, 2019 |access-date=April 22, 2022 |url=https://www.discoverlosangeles.com/visit/rose-bowl-stadium-the-story-of-an-la-icon |archive-date=December 30, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20211230132013/https://www.discoverlosangeles.com/visit/rose-bowl-stadium-the-story-of-an-la-icon |url-status=live }}</ref> The ball for this game was the [[Adidas Questra]], which was introduced at the start of the World Cup.<ref name="tele_Fifa">{{Cite web |title=Fifa World Cup match balls through time |url-status=dead |work=[[The Daily Telegraph]] |date=November 27, 2009 |access-date=November 30, 2009 |url= http://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/world-cup-2010/pictures/6670565/Fifa-World-Cup-match-balls-through-time.html?image=22 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20091130205759/http://www.telegraph.co.uk/sport/football/world-cup-2010/pictures/6670565/Fifa-World-Cup-match-balls-through-time.html?image=22 |archive-date=November 30, 2009}}</ref> The ball was lighter than those previously used. Canadian journalist Scott McKeen described it as having been "engineered to created more excitement for soccer, in a land where there has been little soccer excitement".<ref>{{Cite web |date=July 8, 1994 |page=1 |newspaper=[[Edmonton Journal]] |last=McKeen |first=Scott |via=[[Newspapers.com]] |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/100242146/ |title=Questra adds bounce, but givers players fits |access-date=April 22, 2022 |archive-date=May 9, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220509061114/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/100242146/edmonton-journal/ |url-status=live }}</ref> The ball's controllability was praised by strikers in the tournament, but goalkeepers criticized what they said was its unpredictable tendency to deviate in flight.<ref name="tele_Fifa"/>

[[Brazil national football team|Brazil]] had won the title three times before—[[1958 FIFA World Cup|in 1958]], [[1962 FIFA World Cup|1962]], and [[1970 FIFA World Cup|1970]]—while Italy had also recorded three wins in the [[1934 FIFA World Cup|1934]], [[1938 FIFA World Cup|1938]], and [[1982 FIFA World Cup|1982]] tournaments.<ref name="Britanica"/> The winner would therefore be the first nation to record a fourth World Cup win.<ref>{{Cite web |page=36 |work=[[The News-Press|News-Press]] |last=Wilner |first=Barry |title=Italy, Brazil battle for global power |agency=[[Associated Press]] |via=[[Newspapers.com]] |date=July 17, 1994 |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/100253447/news-press/ |access-date=April 22, 2022 |archive-date=April 22, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220422172748/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/100253447/news-press/ |url-status=live }}</ref> The two teams had met four times previously in the World Cup with two wins each – in the semifinal of the 1938 tournament, which Italy won 2–1; [[1970 FIFA World Cup final|the 1970 final]], which Brazil won 4–1; the match for third place in 1978, won 2–1 by Brazil; and four years later in the group stage, where Italy won the game 3–2.<ref name="11v11FraVsBra">{{Cite web |title=Italy national football team: record v Brazil |author= |work=11v11 |publisher=AFS Enterprises |date= |access-date=October 29, 2021 |url=https://www.11v11.com/teams/italy/tab/opposingTeams/opposition/Brazil/ |archive-date=April 22, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220422180343/https://www.11v11.com/teams/italy/tab/opposingTeams/opposition/Brazil/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=World Cup 1938 finals |author= |access-date=April 22, 2022 |website=[[RSSSF]] |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tables/38full.html |archive-date=June 29, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160629194457/http://www.rsssf.com/tables/38full.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=World Cup 1970 finals |author= |access-date=April 22, 2022 |website=[[RSSSF]] |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tables/70full.html |archive-date=July 1, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160701154217/http://www.rsssf.com/tables/70full.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=World Cup 1978 finals |author= |access-date=April 22, 2022 |website=[[RSSSF]] |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tables/78full.html |archive-date=December 7, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221207104128/https://www.rsssf.org/tables/78full.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |title=World Cup 1982 finals |author= |access-date=April 22, 2022 |website=[[RSSSF]] |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tables/82full.html |archive-date=July 1, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160701154256/http://www.rsssf.com/tables/82full.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

Brazil began the tournament as the favorite, with both pundits and [[bookmaker]]s regarding it as the most likely team to win. Commentators cited the team's star players such as [[Raí]] and [[Romario]], as well as the hot weather conditions, which they said would favor Latin American teams.<ref name="Michele">{{Cite web |title=Brazil seeks ultimate glory |page=33 |work=[[Albuquerque Journal]] |agency=[[Orange County Register]] |last=Himmelberg |first=Michele |via=[[Newspapers.com]] |date=June 17, 1994 |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/100253638/albuquerque-journal/ |access-date=April 22, 2022 |archive-date=April 22, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220422173656/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/100253638/albuquerque-journal/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Italy was also listed before the tournament among the teams likely to win the competition, although some commentators thought it might face difficulties based on poor recent performance and a failure to adapt to manager [[Arrigo Sacchi]]'s preferred attacking style of play.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Group E |page=41 |newspaper=[[The Desert Sun]] |via=[[Newspapers.com]] |date=June 17, 1994 |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/100254630/the-desert-sun/ |access-date=April 22, 2022 |archive-date=April 22, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220422180235/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/100254630/the-desert-sun/ |url-status=live }}</ref> In the 1990 World Cup, Brazil had been eliminated in the round of 16, losing to [[Argentina national football team|Argentina]], while Italy finished third in the tournament, on home soil, beating [[England national football team|England]] in the match for third place after losing to Argentina in a [[penalty shootout]] in the semifinal.<ref>{{Cite web |title=World Cup 1990 |last1=Stokkermans |first1=Karel |last2=Jarreta |first2=Sergio Henrique |date=July 5, 2018 |access-date=April 22, 2022 |website=[[RSSSF]] |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tables/90f.html |archive-date=July 20, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220720114856/https://www.rsssf.org/tables/90f.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

