Brazil was in [[1994 FIFA World Cup Group B|Group B]] at the World Cup, joined by [[Cameroon national football team|Cameroon]], [[Russia national football team|Russia]], and [[Sweden men's national football team|Sweden]].<ref name="RSSSF94">{{Cite web |title=World Cup 1994 finals |author= |date= |access-date=March 11, 2022 |website=[[RSSSF]] |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tables/94full.html |archive-date=December 19, 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20171219031509/http://www.rsssf.com/tables/94full.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Its first game was the tournament opener at [[Stanford Stadium]] in California, on June 20 against Russia.<ref name="GuardianBrazRus">{{cite web |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/97520970/ |via=[[Newspapers.com]] |work=[[The Guardian]] |first=Paul |last=Wilson |title=Brazil ease quickly into their stride |page=18 |date=June 21, 1994 |access-date=April 22, 2022 |archive-date=March 13, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220313110140/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/97520970/the-guardian/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Brazil opened the scoring after 26 minutes, when [[Romário]] evaded his [[Marking (association football)|marker]] following an in-swinging [[corner kick|corner]] and hit the ball into the goal.<ref name="GuardianBrazRus"/><ref name="FIFABrazRus">{{Cite web |title=1994 FIFA World Cup USA : Brazil – Russia |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |website=FIFA.com |date=June 20, 1994 |access-date=July 12, 2025 |url=https://www.fifa.com/en/match-centre/match/17/84/337/3057 |archive-date=October 14, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251014082549/https://www.fifa.com/en/match-centre/match/17/84/337/3057 |url-status=live }}</ref> Brazil scored again after 52 minutes, when [[Raí]] converted a [[Penalty kick (association football)|penalty]] following a foul by [[Vladislav Ternavsky]] on Romário, and went on to complete a 2–0 win.<ref name="FIFABrazRus"/><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/97521025/ |via=[[Newspapers.com]] |work=[[The Boston Globe]] |first=John |last=Powers |author-link=John Powers (journalist) |title=Brazil begins with a win |page=62 |date=June 21, 1998 |access-date=April 22, 2022 |archive-date=March 13, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220313110140/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/97521025/the-san-francisco-examiner/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Its second game was against Cameroon on June 24, once again at Stanford.<ref name="FIFABrazCam">{{Cite web |title=1994 FIFA World Cup USA : Brazil – Cameroon |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |website=FIFA.com|date=June 24, 1994 |access-date=July 12, 2025 |url=https://www.fifa.com/en/match-centre/match/17/84/337/3067}}</ref> Romário gave Brazil the lead after 39 minutes after receiving a pass from [[Dunga]] and hitting it past goalkeeper [[Joseph-Antoine Bell]].<ref name="GuardianBrazCam">{{cite web |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/97600191/ |via=[[Newspapers.com]] |work=[[The Guardian]] |first=Paul |last=Wilson |title=Brazil ease into next stage |page=18 |date=June 25, 1994 |access-date=April 22, 2022 |archive-date=March 14, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220314144510/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/97600191/the-guardian/ |url-status=live }}</ref> In the second half, Cameroon defender [[Rigobert Song]] was [[Ejection (sports)|sent off]] for a foul on Raí, before [[Márcio Santos]] and [[Bebeto]] added two further goals to complete a 3–0 win for Brazil.<ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/97600387/ |via=[[Newspapers.com]] |work=[[Detroit Free Press]] |title=Brazil 3, Cameroon 0 |page=5B |date=June 25, 1994 |access-date=April 22, 2022 |archive-date=March 14, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220314144509/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/97600387/detroit-free-press/ |url-status=live }}</ref> The result meant that Brazil had qualified for the round of 16 with one game to spare.<ref name="GuardianBrazCam"/> Brazil's final group game was against Sweden at the [[Pontiac Silverdome]] in Michigan on June 28. [[Kennet Andersson]] gave Sweden the lead after 23 minutes, following a long pass by [[Tomas Brolin]], before Romário equalized for Brazil with his third goal of the tournament shortly after halftime. The game finished 1–1, which was sufficient for Brazil to finish as group winner.<ref name="FIFABrazSwe">{{Cite web |title=1994 FIFA World Cup USA : Brazil – Sweden |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |website=FIFA.com |date=June 28, 1994 |access-date=July 12, 2025 |url=https://www.fifa.com/en/match-centre/match/17/84/337/3080 |archive-date=October 14, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251014043309/https://www.fifa.com/en/match-centre/match/17/84/337/3080 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="GuardianBrazSwe">{{cite web |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/97600580/ |via=[[Newspapers.com]] |work=[[The Guardian]] |first=David |last=Lacey |title=Romario the great leveller |page=20 |date=June 29, 1994 |access-date=April 22, 2022 |archive-date=March 14, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220314144349/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/97600580/the-guardian/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

Brazil faced the host [[United States men's national soccer team|United States]] in [[1994 FIFA World Cup knockout stage|the round of 16]] on July 4, [[Independence Day (United States)|American Independence Day]]. Brazil's performance through most of the game was described by ''[[The Guardian]]''{{'s}} Paul Wilson as "wholly unconvincing as aspiring champions", until Bebeto scored the game's only goal after 72 minutes.<ref name="GuardianBrazUSA">{{cite web |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/97607421/ |via=[[Newspapers.com]] |work=[[The Guardian]] |first=Paul |last=Wilson |title=Bebeto spoils America's day |page=20 |date=July 5, 1994 |access-date=April 22, 2022 |archive-date=March 14, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220314191123/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/97607421/the-guardian/ |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="FIFABrazUSA">{{Cite web |title=1994 FIFA World Cup USA : Brazil – USA |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |website=FIFA.com |date=July 4, 1994 |access-date=July 12, 2025 |url=https://www.fifa.com/en/match-centre/match/17/84/338/3090 |archive-date=December 6, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251206045637/https://www.fifa.com/en/match-centre/match/17/84/338/3090 |url-status=live }}</ref> Both teams finished the game with 10 players after Brazil's [[Leonardo Araújo|Leonardo]] was dismissed for a violent foul while American [[Fernando Clavijo]] was sent off for a second bookable offense.<ref name="FIFABrazUSA"/> In the quarterfinals, Brazil played [[Netherlands national football team|the Netherlands]] at the [[Cotton Bowl (stadium)|Cotton Bowl]] in Dallas on July 9.<ref name="FIFABrazNeth">{{Cite web |title=1994 FIFA World Cup USA : Netherlands – Brazil |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |website=FIFA.com |date=July 9, 1994 |access-date=July 12, 2025 |url=https://www.fifa.com/en/match-centre/match/17/84/796/3098 |archive-date=December 6, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251206050209/https://www.fifa.com/en/match-centre/match/17/84/796/3098 |url-status=live }}</ref> After a goalless first half, Romário gave Brazil the lead from a Bebeto pass after 53 minutes, before Bebeto himself scored a second ten minutes later.<ref name="FIFABrazNeth"/><ref name="BaltimoreSunBrazNeth">{{cite web |url=https://www.baltimoresun.com/news/bs-xpm-1994-07-10-1994191168-story.html |first=John |last=Powers |author-link=John Powers (journalist) |agency=[[Boston Globe]] |via=[[Newspapers.com]] |work=[[The Baltimore Sun]] |title=Brazil, Italy pull back from edge |pages=[https://www.newspapers.com/clip/97377289/ 1C], [https://www.newspapers.com/clip/97618616/ 12C] |date=July 10, 1994 |access-date=April 22, 2022 |archive-date=April 21, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220421201553/https://www.baltimoresun.com/news/bs-xpm-1994-07-10-1994191168-story.html |url-status=live }}</ref> The Dutch players disputed the second goal, saying that Romário had been caught by their [[offside trap]] when [[Branco (footballer)|Branco]] passed forward to him in the build-up, but the goal stood. [[Rob Witschge]] of the Netherlands later said that the [[Assistant referee (association football)|linesman]] was "absolutely blind".<ref>{{cite web |agency=[[Los Angeles Times]] |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/97619208/ |via=[[Newspapers.com]] |work=[[The Guardian]] |first=Mike |last=Penner |author-link=Mike Penner |title=Dutch Wait, but Whistle Stays Silent |page=C9 |date=July 10, 1994 |access-date=April 22, 2022 |archive-date=March 14, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220314193121/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/97619208/the-los-angeles-times/ |url-status=live }}</ref> The Netherlands pulled a goal back a minute later through [[Dennis Bergkamp]], and then equalized with 14 minutes remaining, through [[Aron Winter]]. Brazil scored again after 81 minutes when Branco hit a free kick from {{convert|30|yards|m}} to seal a 3–2 win.<ref name="FIFABrazNeth"/><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/viewer/bl/0002321/19940710/545/0046 |via=[[British Newspaper Archive]] |work=[[Sunday Tribune]] |title=Brazil Win Epic Battle |first=Paul |last=Radford |date=July 10, 1994 |url-access=subscription |access-date=April 22, 2022 |archive-date=March 19, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220319110045/https://www.britishnewspaperarchive.co.uk/account/register?countrykey=0&showgiftvoucherclaimingoptions=false&gift=false&nextpage=%2Faccount%2Flogin%3Freturnurl%3D%2Fviewer%2Fbl%2F0002321%2F19940710%2F545%2F0046&rememberme=false&cookietracking=false&partnershipkey=0&newsletter=false&offers=false&registerreason=none&showsubscriptionoptions=false&showcouponmessaging=false&showfreetrialmessaging=false&showregisteroptions=false&showloginoptions=false&isonlyupgradeable=false |url-status=live }}</ref> Brazil's semifinal was against Sweden at the [[Rose Bowl (stadium)|Rose Bowl]] in [[Pasadena, California]], on July 13.<ref name="FIFABrazSwed">{{Cite web |title=1994 FIFA World Cup USA : Sweden – Brazil |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |website=FIFA.com |date=July 13, 1994 |access-date=July 12, 2025 |url=https://www.fifa.com/en/match-centre/match/17/84/3461/3099 |archive-date=December 6, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251206045532/https://www.fifa.com/en/match-centre/match/17/84/3461/3099 |url-status=live }}</ref> Sweden defended deeply throughout the game and was reduced to 10 players when Jonas Thern was sent off in the 63rd minute for a foul on Dunga. Brazil had much of the possession and several chances throughout the game, but did not score until the 81st minute when Romário [[Header (association football)|headed]] the ball into the Swedish goal from a [[Jorginho (footballer, born 1964)|Jorginho]] cross. Brazil won 1–0 and earned a place in the final.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/97924627/ |work=[[The Guardian]] |first=Paul |last=Wilson |title=Romario sets up the final carnival |via=[[Newspapers.com]] |page=24 |date=July 14, 1994 |archive-date=March 19, 2022 |access-date=April 22, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220319110038/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/97924627/the-guardian/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

