Italy was drawn in [[1994 FIFA World Cup Group E|Group E]] at the finals, alongside [[Mexico national football team|Mexico]], [[Norway national football team|Norway]], and the [[Republic of Ireland national football team|Republic of Ireland]].<ref name="RSSSF94"/> It began its campaign on June 18 against the Republic of Ireland at [[Giants Stadium]] in [[East Rutherford, New Jersey]]. Italy was one of the pre-tournament favorites to win the World Cup,<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/97395161/ |work=[[Sydney Morning Herald]] |title=Saturday Pick |at=Spectrum p. 20A |date=June 18, 1994 |archive-date=March 11, 2022 |access-date=April 22, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220311180719/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/97395161/the-sydney-morning-herald/ |url-status=live }}</ref> but the crowd was predominantly Irish, many of them [[Irish Americans]]. Ireland won the game 1–0 through an 11th-minute goal by [[Ray Houghton]],<ref name="DellApa">{{cite news |first=Frank |last=Dell'Apa |work=[[The Boston Globe]] |title=Irish have reason to smile – they upset Italy |via=[[Newspapers.com]] |pages=[https://www.newspapers.com/clip/97377289/ 65], [https://www.newspapers.com/clip/97377289/ 68] |date=June 19, 1994}}</ref> a result later described by ''[[The Irish Post]]'' as "one of Ireland's greatest ever sporting moments".<ref>{{Cite web |title=Twenty-five years ago Ray Houghton's goal against Italy shook the Irish nation |last=Brent |first=Harry |work=[[The Irish Post]] |date=June 21, 2019 |access-date=March 11, 2022 |url=https://www.irishpost.com/sport/twenty-five-years-ago-ray-houghtons-goal-italy-shook-irish-nation-168323 |archive-date=December 19, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201219093210/https://www.irishpost.com/sport/twenty-five-years-ago-ray-houghtons-goal-italy-shook-irish-nation-168323 |url-status=live }}</ref> Italy's second game was against Norway on June 23, once again at Giants Stadium. Goalkeeper [[Gianluca Pagliuca]] was sent off after 22 minutes for a [[professional foul]], but Italy recovered to win the game 1–0, [[Dino Baggio]] scoring the winner after 69 minutes.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/97408762/ |work=[[Hartford Courant]] |title=Cup update: Thursday's highlights |via=[[Newspapers.com]] |page=F7 |date=June 24, 1994 |archive-date=November 24, 2022 |access-date=April 22, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221124020817/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/97408762/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Its final group game, against Mexico at [[Robert F. Kennedy Memorial Stadium|RFK Stadium]] in Washington, D.C., began with all four Group E teams level on points.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/97448214/ |title=Cup at a glance |work=[[The Fresno Bee]] |page=C15 |date=June 28, 1994 |archive-date=March 12, 2022 |access-date=April 22, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220312132719/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/97448214/the-fresno-bee/ |url-status=live }}</ref> The Italian supporters were again outnumbered by the opposition, but their team took the lead shortly after halftime on a low shot by [[Daniele Massaro]]. Massaro almost added a second after 56 minutes, but then Mexico equalized a minute later through [[Marcelino Bernal]].<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/97448532/ |work=[[The Guardian]] |first=Paul |last=Wilson |title=Sluggish Italians scrape through |via=[[Newspapers.com]] |page=20 |date=June 29, 1994 |archive-date=March 12, 2022 |access-date=April 22, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220312132719/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/97448532/the-guardian/ |url-status=live }}</ref> The game finished 1–1, while Norway and the Republic of Ireland also tied, leaving all four teams on 4 points with identical [[goal difference]]. With the most goals scored, Mexico was first in the group, while Norway was bottom with fewest goals. Italy and the Republic of Ireland both had two goals each, but Ireland took second place through its win over Italy. Italy qualified for the next round, however, as one of the better-performing third-place teams, with its progress confirmed when Cameroon was defeated by Russia later that day.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/97924675/ |work=[[North County Blade-Citizen]] |agency=[[Associated Press]] |title=Mexico advances with 1–1 tie against Italy |via=[[Newspapers.com]] |page=C-6 |date=June 29, 1994 |archive-date=November 24, 2022 |access-date=April 22, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20221124020817/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/97924675/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

Italy's round of 16 opponent was [[Nigeria national football team|Nigeria]], at [[Foxboro Stadium]] in [[Foxborough, Massachusetts]], on July 5.<ref name="FIFAItalyNigeria">{{Cite web |title=1994 FIFA World Cup USA : Nigeria – Italy |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |website=FIFA.com |date=July 5, 1994 |access-date=July 12, 2025 |url=https://www.fifa.com/en/match-centre/match/17/84/338/3091 |archive-date=December 6, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251206045527/https://www.fifa.com/en/match-centre/match/17/84/338/3091 |url-status=live }}</ref> Nigeria took the lead after 25 minutes, when [[Finidi George]] took a corner which bounced off Italian defender [[Paolo Maldini]] before being struck into the goal by [[Emmanuel Amunike]].<ref name="FIFAItalyNigeria"/> After 75 minutes, Italy's [[Gianfranco Zola]], who had come on as a substitute, was sent off for a foul on [[Augustine Eguavoen]], immediately after he had been denied a penalty.<ref name="FIFAItalyNigeria"/><ref>{{cite web |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/97964781/ |title=Despite a red card, Italians shake upset bid |work=[[The Orlando Sentinel]] |page=D-5 |last=Gola |first=Hank |date=July 6, 1994 |via=[[Newspapers.com]] |access-date=April 22, 2022 |archive-date=March 19, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220319224920/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/97964781/the-orlando-sentinel/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Despite this setback, Italy equalized after 88 minutes when [[Roberto Baggio]] hit a low shot past the Nigerian goalkeeper. With the score at 1–1, the game went to [[Overtime (sports)|extra time]] and the game was decided when Roberto Baggio scored his second goal after 102 minutes, sealing a 2–1 Italian win.<ref name="FIFAItalyNigeria"/><ref name="GuardianItalyNigeria">{{cite web |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/97962610/ |via=[[Newspapers.com]] |work=[[The Guardian]] |first=David |last=Lacey |title=Baggio awakes to end African dream |page=20 |date=July 6, 1994 |access-date=April 22, 2022 |archive-date=March 19, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220319224626/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/97962610/the-guardian/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Italy's quarterfinal game was against [[Spain national football team|Spain]] in Foxborough on July 9. Dino Baggio gave Italy the lead after 25 minutes, before Spain's [[José Luis Caminero]] equalized shortly before the hour mark.<ref name="FIFAItalySpain">{{Cite web |title=1994 FIFA World Cup USA : Italy – Spain |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |website=FIFA.com |date=July 9, 1994 |access-date=July 12, 2025 |url=https://www.fifa.com/en/match-centre/match/17/84/796/3097 |archive-date=December 6, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251206045532/https://www.fifa.com/en/match-centre/match/17/84/796/3097 |url-status=live }}</ref> For the second game in a row, Roberto Baggio scored after 88 minutes, scoring from a tight angle following a run into the [[penalty area]], and Italy won 2–1.<ref>{{Cite web |date=July 10, 1994 |work=[[Fort Worth Star-Telegram]] |via=[[Newspapers.com]] |last=Robertson |first=Linda |title=Baggio lifts Italy past Spain, 2–1 |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/100173128/ |access-date=April 22, 2022 |archive-date=April 21, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220421130151/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/100173128/fort-worth-star-telegram/ |url-status=live }}</ref> It returned to Giants Stadium on July 13 for a semifinal against [[Bulgaria national football team|Bulgaria]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=1994 FIFA World Cup USA : Bulgaria – Italy |publisher=Fédération Internationale de Football Association |website=FIFA.com |date=July 13, 1994 |access-date=July 12, 2025 |url=https://www.fifa.com/en/match-centre/match/17/84/3461/3100 |archive-date=December 6, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251206050006/https://www.fifa.com/en/match-centre/match/17/84/3461/3100 |url-status=live }}</ref> Bulgaria had defeated previous champions [[Argentina national football team|Argentina]] and [[Germany national football team|Germany]] en route to the match, but Italy started strongly, Roberto Baggio scoring twice in the first half hour for a 2–0 lead. Bulgaria's [[Hristo Stoichkov]] pulled a goal back through a penalty shortly before halftime, and protested about two denied penalty claims in the second half, but Italy held on for a 2–1 win and a place in the final.<ref>{{Cite web |title=World Cup '94; Italians Save Their Best for the Cup's Next to Last |last=Mifflin |first=Lawrie |work=[[New York Times]] |date=July 14, 1994 |access-date=April 22, 2022 |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1994/07/14/sports/world-cup-94-italians-save-their-best-for-the-cup-s-next-to-last.html |archive-date=April 22, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220422081901/https://www.nytimes.com/1994/07/14/sports/world-cup-94-italians-save-their-best-for-the-cup-s-next-to-last.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

==Match==
===Summary===
[[File:Arnaldo - Copa.jpg|thumb|Brazilian radio personality Arnaldo Santos at the game. The penalty shootout score is marked on the [[scoreboard]] behind him.]]
Brazil's [[Mazinho]] was booked in the fourth minute for a foul on [[Nicola Berti]]. In the 13th minute, Romario had a chance to score with a header from close range, following a cross by Dunga, but his header went straight to the goalkeeper. Four minutes later, Romario passed the ball through to Bebeto on the left side of the penalty area. Instead of shooting, Bebeto attempted a cross, but it deflected behind off [[Paolo Maldini]].<ref name="RobKingReport">{{Cite web |title=World Cup giants fail to break deadlock |page=26 |work=[[The Birmingham Post]] |first=Rob |last=King |via=[[Newspapers.com]] |date=July 18, 1994 |access-date=April 23, 2022 |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/100304150/the-birmingham-post/ |archive-date=April 23, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220423150550/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/100304150/the-birmingham-post/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Italy then had one of its best chances of the game when [[Daniele Massaro]] beat two defenders and was [[Glossary of association football terms#O|one-on-one]] with Taffarel, but his shot was directed straight at the Brazilian goalkeeper.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Players hope victory lifts Brazil's morale |page=9B |work=[[The Miami Herald]] |last=Le Batard |first=Dan |via=[[Newspapers.com]] |date=July 19, 1994 |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/100363475/the-miami-herald/ |access-date=April 28, 2022 |archive-date=April 24, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220424133835/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/100363475/the-miami-herald/ |url-status=live }}</ref> After 21 minutes, Brazil's [[Jorginho (footballer, born 1964)|Jorginho]] had to leave the game following an injury sustained a few minutes previously and was replaced by [[Cafu]]. Italy made a replacement after 34 minutes, bringing [[Luigi Apolloni]] on for [[Roberto Mussi]], moving Maldini to the left back position. Roberto Baggio attempted a pass through to [[Antonio Benarrivo]] late in the first half but [[Aldair]] was able to intercept it. Late in the half, Brazil had two further opportunities, first from Romario, whose shot was parried by [[Gianluca Pagliuca]] without falling to a Brazilian player, and then through [[Branco (footballer)|Branco]] whose free kick was saved. The free kick resulted from a foul by Apolloni on Romario, for which Apolloni received a [[yellow card (sport)|yellow card]], while Italian defender [[Demetrio Albertini]] was also booked for not being sufficiently far from the ball during the free kick.<ref name="RobKingReport"/> The first half concluded with the score at 0–0.

In the second half, Cafu hit a shot that went across the face of the Italian goal, after which Bebeto attempted to score with a header which was saved by Pagliuca and was also flagged for [[offside (association football)|offside]].<ref name="RobKingReport"/><ref name="Downey">{{cite web |title=This L.A. Jolt Is Felt All Over The World |first=Mike |last=Downey |page=C14 |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/100455912/ |via=[[Newspapers.com]] |work=[[The Los Angeles Times]] |date=July 18, 1994 |access-date=April 28, 2022 |archive-date=April 25, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220425210153/https://www.newspapers.com/clip/100455912/the-los-angeles-times/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Roberto Baggio had a chance for Italy, when he received a pass from Dino Baggio and was able to run at the Brazilian goal, but [[Márcio Santos]] tackled him and kicked the ball out, ending his run.<ref name="RobKingReport"/> Silva attempted a top-spin shot from distance which bounced off the goalpost, after which Mazinho also had a chance but Pagliuca was able to stop the ball.<ref name="Downey"/> Other than that, the second half was largely devoid of scoring chances, as Brazil was unable to break through the center-back pairing of [[Franco Baresi]] and Paolo Maldini. The lack of attacking play in the game was down to strong holding midfield play by Dino Baggio for Italy, and by both Dunga and [[Mauro Silva]] for Brazil.

As extra time began, chances became more plentiful as Brazil began to dominate play. Romário had another chance early in extra time after Pagliuca made an ill-advised decision to come for a cross, leaving the goal exposed, but Bebeto was unable to find him. Roberto Baggio, who had been quiet for most of the game after having picked up an injury earlier in the tournament, had his only sight of goal in the entire game in the first half of extra time, hitting a shot from around 25 yards, but Taffarel pushed the shot over. [[Zinho]] had a chance on the stroke of halftime in extra time, hitting his shot well from inside the area, but Pagliuca saved well at his near post. In the second half of extra time, Romário missed a golden chance to hand Brazil the trophy, as [[Cafu]] had found him inside the six yard box. With Pagliuca at least four yards away from him on the other side of the goal, and the entire goal to aim at, the off balance Romário put his shot wide of the post.

This was the first time the World Cup was decided on penalties. Italian captain Baresi took the first penalty and blazed his penalty over the bar. Brazilian center-back Márcio Santos went next, but Pagliuca saved the Brazilian defender's effort low to his right. [[Demetrio Albertini]] went next for Italy, scoring his penalty into the top corner. Romário was next for Brazil, and he scored his penalty, placing it so far in the corner that it went in off the post, tying the score at 1–1. [[Alberico Evani]] went third for Italy, he scored, putting his penalty high and in the middle, as Taffarel dove to the right. Branco went next for Brazil, and he scored in the bottom left corner. Massaro, who had scored twice in the Champions League Final less than two months previously, went next for Italy, but he was unable to put the ball in Taffarel's net just as he had been unable to do in the game, as the Brazilian keeper saved to his left. Brazilian captain Dunga was next, and he comfortably placed his shot into the bottom left corner, making it game point. With Italy needing to score to keep its dreams alive, talisman Roberto Baggio stepped up to take the penalty. In what would become an infamous moment in World Cup history, Baggio blazed his penalty over the bar, in similar fashion to Baresi, and giving Brazil their fourth title.<ref name="FIFA"/>

